From 37f7c8a4a503f48fe67f4aea2b65adff4df39b38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:44:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(geo): the one sentence every page exists to state was
 unliftable by a string extractor
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FOUND BY A CURL THAT CAME BACK EMPTY

Two live checks, run side by side after the New York deploy:

  curl .../service-areas/pittsford | grep -o 'Ecowoods serves Pittsford'  →  nothing
  curl .../service-areas/buffalo   | grep -o 'The showroom is Toronto'    →  matched

The second matched because it is one uninterrupted static chunk. The first did
not, and the sentence is on the page.

WHY

React's hydratable renderer separates adjacent text and expression children:

  React.createElement('p', null, 'Ecowoods serves ', name, '. Book the measure.')
  → <p>Ecowoods serves <!-- -->Pittsford, NY<!-- -->. Book the measure.</p>

  React.createElement('p', null, `Ecowoods serves ${name}. Book the measure.`)
  → <p>Ecowoods serves Pittsford, NY. Book the measure.</p>

Verified against react-dom/server rather than assumed: renderToStaticMarkup
produces neither separator, which is why this is invisible in a unit test and
visible only in what the site actually serves.

A browser reads both the same. Any crawler that parses HTML into a tree reads
both the same. Something that reads the response as a string — which several of
the simpler answer-engine fetchers do — reads the first as three fragments and
cannot lift the sentence. That sentence is the entire reason eighty-nine service
pages exist, and on the twenty-six New York pages it is doing the heaviest work
on the site: it is what says this company takes the job and where the shop is.

Both required sentences are now single template literals: one text node each,
contiguous in the served HTML.

THE CHECK

verify-geo-green gains a seventh refusal, on the source rather than the output:
these two sentences must be interpolated strings, not JSX children. A future
edit that tidies them back into JSX — which is the more natural way to write
them, and which is how they were written — fails the build. Regression run and
observed.

66 guards green, 317 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
